---
import Chapter from "../layouts/Chapter.astro"
import Aside from "../components/Aside.astro"
import Code from "../components/Code.astro"
import { components } from "../lib/catalog"
import { componentDocs } from "../lib/docs"

const button = componentDocs("button")
---

<Chapter
  title="Introduction"
  part="Guide"
  kicker="Chapter 1"
  lede="What gpuicn is, how installing source differs from adding a crate, and what the rest of this book expects you to know."
>
  <div class="text">
    <h2 class="h2">What you install</h2>
    <p>
      gpuicn is a set of {components.length} components for GPUI. Visually they follow shadcn/ui.
      Behaviorally they use GPUI Kit, which supplies pointer, keyboard, and accessibility handling on
      top of GPUI's renderer. A Dialog traps focus and a Slider reads arrow keys. Neither behavior is
      implemented in gpuicn's own files.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Aside>
    <p>
      The pinned revisions are gpui-pre 0.3.10 and GPUI Kit 0.8.1. Apps on another GPUI revision must
      move to these before installing components.
    </p>
  </Aside>
  <div class="text">
    <p>
      You do not depend on gpuicn at runtime. The CLI copies a component's Rust module into your
      crate, where it compiles against the same <code>gpui_kit</code> types your own views use. The
      module is yours after that. Updates never touch it unless you ask with <code>--overwrite</code>.
    </p>
    <p>This is the Usage example for Button, which is the whole public API a view needs:</p>
  </div>
  <Code code={button.usage} />

  <div class="text">
    <h2 class="h2">Source or crate</h2>
    <p>There are two ways to use the catalog. They are meant to be combined.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Installed source</strong> is the default. <code>gpuicn add</code> writes modules under
      <code>src/ui</code> and keeps <code>src/ui/mod.rs</code> current. Every change you make is a
      normal commit in your repository, reviewed like any other code.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Aside>
    <p>
      <code>gpuicn new --crate</code> and <code>gpuicn init --crate</code> set up crate mode;
      <code>gpuicn eject button</code> turns one component back into editable source.
    </p>
  </Aside>
  <div class="text">
    <p>
      <strong>The <code>gpuicn</code> crate</strong> compiles the same registry behind one Cargo
      dependency. It suits an app that wants the whole catalog without files to maintain. When you
      need to change one component, eject it. The ejected file is the same source the crate was built
      from.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h2">How to read a component chapter</h2>
    <p>Each of the {components.length} component chapters has the same five parts.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        A numbered figure that runs the component. The preview is the component itself, compiled to
        WebAssembly, so you can test the behavior before you install it.
      </li>
      <li>Installation, which gives the command and the module declarations the CLI maintains.</li>
      <li>Usage, a compile-checked example to paste into a view.</li>
      <li>API reference, every public function in the installed file, generated from the source.</li>
      <li>
        Platform notes, which record where behavior on this GPUI revision differs from the browser
        original, including accessibility limits.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="h2">What this book assumes</h2>
    <p>
      You can read Rust and have built a GPUI window before. The <a href="/installation/">Installation</a>
      chapter is a how-to, not a tutorial. It names each file it writes and what you should see, but it
      does not explain GPUI's context or entity model. For that, read GPUI's own documentation.
    </p>
    <p>
      gpuicn is experimental. Component APIs and visuals change between releases, and a release may
      change installed source in ways a <code>--dry-run</code> shows before you accept them. Pin one
      release, and read the <a href="https://github.com/karyalabs/gpuicn/releases">release notes</a>
      before moving to the next.
    </p>
  </div>
</Chapter>
